before we get started with the  presentations we are going to focus a  lot on psychotherapy today and it  occurred to me that we haven't actually  introduced the construct of borderline  personality disorder so I wanted to  review the diagnostic criteria and the  general feeling of what the symptoms are  for the disorder before we get started  this will be really brief this is an  extra hand out in your book it's one of  the sheets that came in it BPD and dsm-5  is very similar to BPD and dsm for its  defined as a pervasive pattern of  relationship self-image and effective  instability and marked impulsivity and  in order to meet criteria in the  diagnostic manual for this disorder you  have to have five of the following  criteria frantic efforts to avoid  abandonment unstable or intense  interpersonal relationships identity  disturbance dangerous impulsivity in at  least two areas recurrent suicidal  behavior gestures threats or  self-mutilation effective instability  due to a March reactivity of mood  chronic feelings of emptiness  inappropriate intense anger or  difficulty controlling anger and or  transient stress-related paranoia or  severe dissociative symptoms you'll hear  the presenters talk about these symptoms  today in different treatments and so you  can refer back to these symptoms when  they're talking about it another way to  think about is that there are really  three core areas of dysfunction  associated with the diagnosis unstable  relationships effective dysregulation  and impulse impulsivity or self-control  BPD is fairly common community studies  have found that it's in one to two  percent of the population and some of  them some studies have shown up to six  percent and in terms of clinical  services that can be ten percent of  outpatients than twenty percent of  impatience I don't want to take up too  much more time I just want to say one  thing when i first started studying  psychology as an undergrad i was  introduced to the concept of BPD and it  was introduced to me as a cut as a  disorder from which people never  recovered they never changed and there  was no treatment and we have come a  really long  away from that and I'm so happy to have  all our speakers here today they're  going to speak to the effective  interventions that we now have for the  disorder and the changes that we see for  people with the disorder so I thank them  all for being here today and with that  I'm going to invite dr. Witter whole to  join me
 

